22:59:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've hit on a big, uncomfortable truth: **there was no single, coordinated, globally enforced plan**—at least not one that was followed all the way through. The story is messier than that, and your instinct is mostly correct. Let me unpack it. ### The (theoretical) plan: IPv6 In the early 1990s, the IETF *did* foresee the shortage and started designing IPv6 (then called IPng – Next Generation). By 1998, IPv6 was a formal standard. The *intended* plan was: 1. **Get everyone to deploy IPv6** before IPv4 ran out. 2. **Run both in parallel (dual-stack)** for a transition period. 3. **Eventually turn off IPv4** and live on IPv6 forever. That was the plan. It was sensible. But it **failed** for three reasons: - **No economic incentive.** IPv4 worked fine for most people. Buying new routers, updating software, retraining staff—that costs money, and the payoff was intangible. IPv4 addresses were still free or cheap, so why spend? - **The internet grew explosively.** The number of devices (phones, laptops, IoT) exploded in the 2000s, eating up addresses faster than anyone expected. - **NAT appeared as a surprisingly effective patch.** Instead of dealing with IPv6, companies and ISPs discovered they could hide hundreds of devices behind one IP using NAT. That “solved” the shortage for most users—for a while. ### So what *really* happened? Patching as we went. When the shortage became acute (late 2000s), the “plan” shifted to a **muddle-through** approach: - **NAT became Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT)** – ISPs stacked multiple layers of translation. Not planned, but it worked. - **IPv4 address trading** – A market emerged where used blocks were bought/sold. That's not in any official plan; it's just capitalism solving a resource scarcity. - **IPv6 deployment happened, but slowly and unevenly** – Driven by people who *needed* it (mobile carriers, big cloud providers), not by a master schedule. We're still in transition 25 years later. So yes, we are **patching as we go**. The official "plan" (IPv6) was correct but never enforced or universally adopted. The internet’s growth outpaced governance, and pragmatic workarounds won the day. ### Why that isn’t entirely a bad thing (or entirely a good thing) - **Good:** The internet kept working seamlessly for billions of users. No single day caused a crisis. The “patching” was *resilient*—NAT and trading bought time. - **Bad:** It created fragmentation. IPv6 adoption is still only ~40% globally, CGNAT breaks some applications, the address market is opaque and expensive, and we're still paying the complexity tax decades later. ### The honest takeaway Was there a plan? **Yes, for IPv6.** But there was no global authority to force it. The real-world plan became: *“Use whatever works, and figure out the rest later.”* The IETF, ISPs, and engineers didn't set out to “patch as they go”—but that's exactly what happened, because human systems rarely follow neat blueprints. So your observation is spot on: **We improvised using NAT, markets, and slow IPv6 adoption.** The shortage didn't cause a collapse, but it exposed that the “plan” was more of a suggestion. And we're still living with the consequences of that improvisation.
